  "key": "pr-2452",
  "fields": {
    "summary": "fix(cli): dial wizard routes the deployment fetch through the proxy gate (per-provider network binding)",
    "description": "Refs #249. Companion to the test-speed campaign #2450/#2451 (split out per owner call — prod correctness does not ride a test-perf PR).

## Problem

`/provider dial setup` fetched deployments via a bare `fetchDialModels(baseUrl, key)` — the only dial path NOT routed through `modelFetchClient`. Two consequences:

- a provider bound to a proxy (`providers:<name>.network`, feature #1921) had its wizard model-list traffic go **direct** — a fail-open bypass of the gate (sibling saved-entry bypasses: #249);
- `AgentCliConfig.modelsHttpClient` test injection never applied there — the DIAL setup tests paid 3x15s LIVE network timeouts (found during the test-speed campaign).

## Fix

- `modelFetchClientFor(candidates)`: test injection wins, then the first BOUND candidate name, else null — the direct path, byte-identical legacy behavior for unbound setups.
- `dialWizardClientKeyCandidates(baseUrl)`: the binding may live under the **re-run entry's saved name**, the **host-derived default name** (the name prompt pre-fills it), or the **catalog name** (`dial`) — a literal catalog key alone misses the first two (owner review catch on the first attempt).
- `dialWizardModelFetchClient(baseUrl)` composes the two; `_dialAskDeployment` uses it.

The saved-entry fetches (`provider_models.dart:544/551`, same bypass class, same `entry.name` keying) remain in #249.

## Tests

- candidate order: re-run entry name → host-derived → catalog;
- each binding key rides its proxy (non-null fail-closed client);
- nothing bound → null (direct path);
- `modelsHttpClient` injection wins over every binding (identity assert) — the seam the DIAL trio uses to stay off the network.

57/57 provider tests green; `dart analyze` clean."
  }
}

